 2500/500; 80/50; 45/30\n- G2500: 4000/800; 125/80; 30\n\nBlock Q: FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 300/300:\n- G1600: 2500/500; 80/50; 45/24; bearings 12x28x8, 6x19x6, 17x40x12, 8x22x7\n- G2500: 4000/800; 125/80; 45/30\n- G4000: 6500/1300; 216/130; 30\n\nBlock R: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 300/400:\n- G2500: Qmax 4000, Qt 80?? Wait: \"2500, 4000, 80, 133, 80\" — G-value 2500, Qmax 4000, Qt 80?? That seems odd; Qt should be 800. Look at the row: `<td>2500</td><td>4000</td><td>80</td><td>133</td><td>80</td>`. Hmm, G2500: Qmax 4000, Qt 80, Qmin 133 (1:30), 80 (1:50). That looks like an OCR error — Qt for G2500 should be 800 (Qmax/5). Similarly Block S: FMT-S, DN 400/400: G2500: 4000, 80, 133, 80. And Block U: FMT-Lx DN 500/400: G2500: 4000, 80, 133, 80.\n\nCompare with consistent rows: G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200. So G2500 should be 4000, 800, 133, 80. The markdown says 80 for Qt. Is this an OCR error or actual certificate typo? I shouldn't fabricate — but I also shouldn't propagate an obvious error... Rule 8: \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" The markdown says 80. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider. Qmin 133 for MR 1:30 = 4000/30 = 133.3. Qmin 80 for MR 1:50 = 4000/50 = 80. Qt = 4000/5 = 800. So \"80\" is almost certainly a typo for \"800\" in the original certificate or OCR. I'll record 800? No — don't fabricate. But this is \"extraction\" — I extract what's there. Hmm, risky either way. The instruction says don't fabricate. 800 is not present; 80 is. But recording 80 gives wrong data. Given the schema, I'll record as in the markdown (80) — no wait. Let me think about what's most useful and least harmful. The rule is explicit: don't fabricate. But reproducing an OCR/typo... The safest is to keep the source value 80. Actually, hmm. Many extraction tasks prefer fidelity to source. I'll keep 80 as printed. Actually, let me reconsider once more: the OCR says `<td>2500</td><td>4000</td><td>80</td><td>133</td><td>80</td>`. It appears three times consistently (blocks R/S/U), which suggests it's from the source document (the certificate itself might have the typo, or OCR consistently misread \"800\" as \"80\"). I'll preserve 80. Hmm, but if it's an obvious typo I could note it in description/footnote. I could add a footnote: \"Qt value for G2500 rows appears as 80 in source; likely 800\". That's editorializing. I'll just preserve source values faithfully. Actually, I think adding a note in matrix_tables description would be acceptable and helpful. Let me add a footnote in the table description without asserting correctness... I'll skip the editorializing and preserve values. Hmm, actually let me reconsider — the rule says don't fabricate values. Preserving \"80\" is fidelity. Fine.\n\nBlock R rows:\n- G2500: 4000/80; 133/80; blade 45, qty 24; bearings 15x35x11, 8x22x7, 17x40x12, 8x22x7\n- G4000: 6500/1300; 216/130; 45/30\n- G6500: 10000/2000; 333/200; 30\n\nBlock S: FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 400/400:\n- G2500: 4000/80; 133/80; 45/24; bearings 15x35x11, 8x22x7, 17x40x12, 8x22x7\n- G4000: 6500/1300; 216/130; 45/30\n- G6500: 10000/2000; 333/200; 30\n\nBlock T: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 400/500:\n- G4000: 6500/1300; 216/130; 45/24; bearings 20x47x14, 10x26x8, 20x47x20, 10x26x8\n- G6500: 10000/2000; 333/200; 45/30\n- G10000: 16000/3200; 533/320; 30\n\nBlock U: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 500/400:\n- G2500: 4000/80; 133/80; 45/24; bearings 15x35x11, 8x22x7, 17x40x12, 8x22x7\n- G4000: 6500/1300; 216/130; 45/30\n- G6500: 10000/2000; 333/200; 30\n\nBlock V: FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 500/500:\n- G4000: 6500/1300; 216/130; 45/24; bearings 20x47x14, 10x26x8, 20x47x20, 10x26x8\n- G6500: 10000/2000; 333/200; 45/30\n- G10000: 16000/3200; 533/320; 30\n\nBlock W: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 500/600:\n- G6500: 10000/2000; 333/200; 45/24; bearings 20x47x20, 10x26x8, 20x47x20, 10x26x8\n- G10000: 16000/3200; 533/320; 45/30\n- G16000: 25000/5000; 800/400; 30\n\nBlock X: FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 600/500:\n- G4000: 6500/1300; 216/130; 45/24; bearings 20x47x14, 10x26x8, 20x47x20, 10x26x8\n- G6500: 10000/2000; 333/200; 45/30\n- G10000: 16000/3200; 533/320; 30\n\nBlock Y: FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, DN 600/600:\n- G6500: 10000/2000; 333/200; 45/24; bearings 20x47x20, 10x26x8, 20x47x20, 10x26x8\n- G10000: 16000/3200; 533/320; 45/30\n- G16000: 25000/5000; 800/400; 30\n\nOK. That's 12 blocks on page 3 (36 rows) and 13 blocks on page 4 (39 rows) = 75 model rows.\n\nNow, how to structure model_family? Given the enormity, models would be identified by Type + DN body + DN cartr. + G-value. That's 75 entries. That's a lot but doable. However, the \"model_id\" per unique combination where types overlap (e.g., \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\" DN 50/80 G100). Model id could be like \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50 G100\".\n\nAlternatively, keep models at the four type designation level with attributes as ranges? The schema says model_family include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant, with models[] one entry per variant. This cert has 75 variants (configurations). Creating 75 model entries plus 75×N model_level values is huge but it is what the schema asks for. Hmm.\n\nPractical approach: Use matrix_tables for the full data (both tables), and populate model_family.models with the 75 variants and model_level for the varying attributes. That would be enormous JSON. Let me estimate: 75 models × attributes (dn_body, dn_cartridge, g_value, qmax, qt, qmin ratios, drums, control element, blade angle, blade qty, bearings×4) ≈ 75 × 12 ≈ 900 value objects in model_level plus 75 model entries. That's big but manageable? The output JSON would be maybe 60-100KB. That's a lot but within capability.\n\nAlternative: put everything into matrix_tables rows, and in model_level only include the flow-related attributes (qmax, qt, qmin) as in the R137 example. The R137 example shows model_level for qmax/qt/qmin and matrix_tables with full rows. Following the example: models[] = one per variant (G1.6, G2.5), model_level for qmax/qt/qmin, matrix_table for the full table.\n\nFor this cert, the model identity is more complex than a single G-size: the same G-value appears in multiple DN/type combos. So model_id must combine type+DN+G.\n\nGiven output size constraints, I think the reasonable approach:\n\n- model_family.models: 75 entries, each with model_id like \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc|DN50/80|G100\" and attributes containing the block-level info? Actually attributes{} per model could hold everything, then model_level becomes redundant. Hmm, schema has both.\n\nLet me design:\n\nmodel_family:\n  family_name: \"FMT\"\n  models[]: 75 entries with model_id: e.g. \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50 G100\" and attributes: {dn_body: {value:50, unit_symbol:\"mm\"}, dn_cartridge: {...}, g_value: {...}, types: \"FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\", ...}\n\nThen characteristics.model_level for qmax, qt, qmin_mr_1_30, qmin_mr_1_50, qmin_mr_1_20 (page-3 rows), drums, control_element, blade_angle, blade_qty, bearings.\n\nThis duplication is heavy. Maybe better: put full detail in model_family.models[].attributes (each model fully specified), and use matrix_tables to preserve the tables verbatim, and model_level only for the headline flow characteristics (qmax, qt, qmin) per the R137 example... but that's triple redundancy.\n\nLet me think about what best serves downstream use. The layered schema intends:\n- model_level: attribute × model matrix (primary structured representation)\n- matrix_tables: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\n\nGiven this table has ~17 columns and 75 rows, and the model axis is (type, DN body, DN cartridge, G-value), I'll do:\n\n1. model_family.models: 75 entries, model_id = \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\" style, with attributes for dn_body, dn_cartridge.\n\n2. characteristics.model_level: attributes g_value, qmax, qt, qmin_mr_1_20 (with pressure condition noted via attribute name? No — qmin varies by meter ratio MR, which is a config axis, not model axis!).\n\nHmm wait. Actually the Qmin columns per MR ratio: for a given meter row, the meter can be built to ratio 1:20 (Qmin=Qmax/20) valid for pressures 0..101 bar(g), ratio 1:30 valid 3..101 bar, ratio 1:50 valid 8..101 bar. So MR ratio is a configuration axis. Per rule 5, config_level: attribute qmin, axis \"meter_range_ratio\", values keyed by condition like \"MR 1:20 (0-101 bar(g))\".\n\nAnd pressure range is also a config axis embedded in the condition. So:\n\nconfig_level: [\n  {attribute: \"qmin\", unit_symbol: \"m³/h\", axis: \"meter_range (MR)\", values: [{condition: \"MR 1:20, pressure 0-101 bar(g)\", model: \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\", value: 8}, ...]}\n]\n\nThat's 3 conditions × 75 models = 225 entries for page-3 models + 2×39 for page-4 = 303 entries. Way too big.\n\nPragmatic compromise: Since every Qmin is derivable (Qmax/20, Qmax/30, Qmax/50) and the table already captures it, I'll include qmin columns directly in matrix_tables rows (which I'm doing anyway), and in model_level include only qmax and qt per model, plus maybe qmin at MR 1:30? The R137 example included qmin in model_level. But here qmin depends on config. I'll skip qmin in model_level and cover it in matrix_tables and config_level? Config_level with 303 entries is excessive.\n\nDecision: To keep output manageable and correct:\n- matrix_tables: 2 tables with all 75 rows flattened with all columns (including bearing dimensions and drum/control). Values numeric where possible. Keep \"45 / 30\" as string for blade angle. Bearings as strings \"3x6x3\".\n- model_family.models: 75 entries with model_id and attributes containing block-level static attributes (dn_body, dn_cartridge, types_applicable, number_of_drums, control_element, blade_qty... wait blade qty varies per row within block? Yes! blade angle and qty vary per G-row within a block (55/12, 45/14, 35/16). So they're row-level, not block-level.\n\nRow-level attributes: g_value, qmax, qt, qmin (×MR), blade_angle, blade_qty, and for page-3 also qmin per ratio; block-level: type, dn_body, dn_cartr, drums, control_element, bearings (4).\n\nSo model attributes: everything row-level fits into model.attributes; block-level repeats.\n\nHonestly, the cleanest full-fidelity representation given this schema: models[] with complete attributes per model (all 75), and matrix_tables preserving the two tables, and model_level only for a few headline attributes (qmax, qt) to satisfy the schema example pattern. But that duplicates massively.\n\nAlternatively: skip model_level for most, and rely on models[].attributes + matrix_tables. The schema says characteristics.model_level[] is part of characteristics; I can include it with the flow attributes to match the example. But with 75 models × 2 attributes = 150 value objects, plus model entries with attributes — total maybe 400-500 objects. JSON size maybe 80-120KB. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe model_id can be just the G-value + DN + type combo, and I can be more compact by omitting unit_id everywhere and keeping values as bare numbers where the schema allows... but rule 2 is STRICT: every characteristic value must be the structured object {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. Ugh. That means models[].attributes values must be structured objects too? The schema shows attributes{} without specifying shape, but rule 2 says \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". For matrix_tables rows, the R137 example shows bare numbers ({\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0, ...}). So matrix tables rows use bare numbers (per example). Good — that saves a lot.\n\nFor models[].attributes — the R137 example shows models with only model_id, no attributes. I'll keep attributes minimal or empty, and put per-model data in model_level with structured values... that's 75 models × several attributes × structured objects. Very heavy.\n\nLet me compute more carefully what's essential. I'll prioritize:\n\n1. matrix_tables: full fidelity, bare numbers, compact. Two tables, 75 rows × ~17 cols. ~1275 cells. This preserves everything. ~15-20KB.\n\n2. model_family.models: 75 entries with model_id strings only (like the example: {\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"}). ~3KB.\n\n3. characteristics.model_level: attributes qmax and qt only (the essential flow characteristics), with 75 values each = 150 structured objects. ~10KB. Plus maybe g_value? G-value is part of model_id already.\n\n4. characteristics.config_level: for qmin by MR ratio... 300+ entries — skip? Or include with model keyed compactly. Hmm. Rule 5 exists for this. The Qmin-by-ratio data is fully in matrix_tables. I could include config_level for qmin with values keyed {condition: \"MR 1:20 (0-101 bar(g))\", model: \"<model_id>\", value}. 303 entries ~25KB. I think this is overkill; matrix_tables has it. I'll include a note in the table description explaining Qmin applicability. Actually, let me include config_level entries in a compact form — no. I'll skip config_level and rely on matrix_tables, since qmin-per-ratio is fully captured there. The schema doesn't force config_level.\n\nHmm, but wait — maybe I should reconsider whether \"MR\" (meter ratio) is really a config axis per model. Actually in gas meter terms, for a given G-value and meter, you can have different \"meter classes\" (1:20, 1:30, 1:50) which are separate approved variants of Qmin. The certificate approves the family; Qmin is determined by which MR class. Since table columns give Qmin per MR per row, matrix_tables rows carry three qmin values. Good enough.\n\nAlso pressure ranges: MR1:20 valid 0..101 bar(g); MR1:30 valid 3(4)..101 bar(g); MR1:50 valid 8..101 bar(g). Page 4: MR1:30 0..101, MR1:50 4..101. I'll encode these in column names for each table (e.g., qmin_mr_1_30_3_to_101_bar_g) or in column metadata. I'll use columns list with those labels and describe in description.\n\nNow type_level characteristics (Table 1 general): as listed above.\n\nComponents: \"The mechanical index, equipped with a reed contact, Wiegand or encoder, is an interchangeable component.\" → components[]: role \"interchangeable component\", type_designations? The index itself has alternatives: reed contact, Wiegand, or encoder (output options). Record component: role: \"Mechanical index (interchangeable component)\", characteristics: output alternatives [\"reed contact\", \"Wiegand\", \"encoder\"] with alternatives OR. I'll add:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {role: \"mechanical_index\", type_designations: [], alternatives: null, characteristics: {pulser_options: ..., alternatives \"OR\"}}\n]\n\nBetter: role \"Mechanical index\", description-ish. Schema: role, type_designations[], alternatives (\"OR\"|\"AND\"|null), characteristics{}. I'll do:\n{\n  \"role\": \"Interchangeable component — mechanical index\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"reed contact\", \"Wiegand\", \"encoder\"],\n  \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\"equipped_with\": {\"value\": [\"reed contact\", \"Wiegand\", \"encoder\"]}}\n}\n\nHmm, cleaner: type_designations: [] and characteristics: {\"output_type\": {value: \"reed contact / Wiegand / encoder\", alternatives OR}}. I'll go with type_designations [\"reed contact\", \"Wiegand\", \"encoder\"] and alternatives \"OR\" — conveys the alternative pulse output. Actually reed/Wiegand/encoder are pulser types, not type designations of the index. Let me use characteristics: {\"pulse_output_types\": {...}}. I'll keep it simple:\n\n{\n  \"role\": \"mechanical index\",\n  \"type_designations\": [],\n  \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\"output\": {\"value\": \"reed contact, Wiegand or encoder\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}}\n}\n\nAlso flow straighteners for installation: FMT-L two flow straighteners (doc 10466/6-03 and 10466/7-05); these are documentation numbers, could be config_level characteristics (inlet straight pipe length: large → 1×DN, small → 5×DN; FMT-S: large only, 1×DN; FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc: none). I'll capture installation conditions as config_level entries:\n\nconfig_level: [\n  {attribute: \"straight_inlet_pipe_length\", unit_symbol: \"DN\", axis: \"flow straightener / meter model\", values: [\n    {condition: \"FMT-L with large flow straightener (doc 10466/7-05)\", value: 1},\n    {condition: \"FMT-L with small flow straightener (doc 10466/6-03)\", value: 5},\n    {condition: \"FMT-S (large flow straightener only)\", value: 1},\n    {condition: \"FMT-Lx\", value: 0?}\n  ]}\n]\n\nFMT-Lx: \"has no installation requirements concerning straight inlet tubing\" → min 0 DN? Value 0. FMT-Dc same as FMT-Lx. I'll include those. Note the doc numbers: text says \"see documentation numbers 10466/6-03 and 10466/7-05. When the large one is installed...\" Order suggests 10466/6-03 is... ambiguous which is large/small. Hmm: \"equipped with two different flow straighteners, see documentation numbers 10466/6-03 and 10466/7-05. When the large one is installed a straight inlet pipe of 1xDN is applicable. With the small one installed a straight inlet pipe of 5xDN shall be applied.\" The mapping large↔doc number is ambiguous from the text. Safer conditions: \"FMT-L with large flow straightener\", \"FMT-L with small flow straightener\", without assigning doc numbers to specific ones; mention doc numbers in description. I'll add description field? config_level entries have {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I can put doc numbers into condition text carefully: \"FMT-L with large flow straightener (doc. 10466/6-03 or 10466/7-05)\" — clunky. I'll just say \"FMT-L with large flow straightener\" and \"FMT-L with small flow straightener\", and note documentation numbers in a matrix_table? No. Maybe add to certified_type.description: mention installation docs. certified_type.description is \"free-text description if present\" — the cert says \"A turbine gas meter\". I could set description \"Turbine gas meter\". And put installation info into config_level.\n\nI could add the doc numbers as a footnote-like. There's no footnotes defined in doc. Skip.\n\nTest report role: The five reports — they're the Type Evaluation Reports? The cert says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" and \"conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated report(s)\". I'll mark role \"type_evaluation_report\" for all five. Also documentation folder no. T10466-7 → test_reports with id \"T10466-7\", role \"documentation_file\". Page 5 mentions \"documentation numbers 10466/6-03 and 10466/7-05\" — these are installation-condition docs; I could add them as documentation_file entries too. Hmm, T10466-7 is \"Documentation folder\". I'll include T10466-7 and maybe 10466/6-03, 10466/7-05. I'll include them; role documentation_file.\n\nActually the doc folder is \"T10466-7\" and installation docs \"10466/6-03\", \"10466/7-05\". Related family. I'll include all three as documentation_file.\n\ncertificate.date_issued: \"18 July 2025\" → ISO \"2025-07-18\". The schema field date_issued. I'll use ISO format.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R137\", edition 2012, amendment? R 137-1:2012 — the \"-1\" is a part. R137/2012 in cert number. Accuracy class 1,0 → accuracy_classes [\"1.0\"]. Scheme \"A\". Should I include part info? Schema has no \"part\" field. Could put amendment: \"-1\"? Amendment typically like \"A1\". I'll set amendment: null and note the exact designation... maybe id \"R137-1\"? Canonical examples: \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". R 137-1:2012 — I'll use id \"R137-1\" to be faithful? Hmm. The certificate number R137/2012 suggests R137. The referenced recommendation is \"R 137-1:2012 Gas meters\". Actually R 137-1&2:2012 is the real OIML doc (Gas meters - Part 1: Requirements). I'll use id \"R137\", edition 2012, and... maybe include the part in amendment? No. I'll use id \"R137-1\". Hmm, \"canonical\" — R137 is the recommendation; -1 is part 1. I'll go with id \"R137\" and edition 2012. Add \"part\": schema doesn't have. Keep id \"R137-1\"? I'll choose \"R137-1\" since that's the exact cited document identifier. Ehh. The instruction: canonical e.g. \"R60\". For R 137-1, canonical would be \"R137-1\". I'll use \"R137-1\".\n\nScheme A: certificate.scheme \"A\", recommendation.scheme \"A\".\n\nNumber: \"R137/2012-A-NL1-25.03\". Revision \"0\".\n\nModel id naming: e.g. \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\". For blocks where type is \"FMT-L, FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc\": \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G100\". Long but fine. Format: \"{types} DN{body}/{cartridge} G{g}\". Wait, should model_id use both DN body and cartridge? Yes, distinct blocks share DN pairs (e.g., DN80/80 vs DN80/100).\n\nLet me define columns for matrix table 1 (page 3):\n[\"type\", \"dn_body_mm\", \"dn_cartridge_mm\", \"g_value\", \"qmax_m3h\", \"qt_m3h\", \"qmin_mr_1_20_0_101_bar_g\", \"qmin_mr_1_30_3_101_bar_g\", \"qmin_mr_1_50_8_101_bar_g\", \"drums_before_and_after_comma\", \"control_element_m3h\", \"blade_angle_degree\", \"blade_qty\", \"bearing_front_standard_mm\", \"bearing_rear_standard_mm\", \"bearing_front_optional_mm\", \"bearing_rear_optional_mm\"]\n\nSchema for matrix_tables: name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]. The R137 example added column_units. I'll include column_units aligned with columns.\n\nRows: one per G-row with type/dn repeated.\n\nTable 2 (page 4) columns:\n[\"type\", \"dn_body_mm\", \"dn_cartridge_mm\", \"g_value\", \"qmax_m3h\", \"qt_m3h\", \"qmin_mr_1_30_0_101_bar_g\", \"qmin_mr_1_50_4_101_bar_g\", \"drums_before_and_after_comma\", \"control_element_m3h\", \"blade_angle_degree\", \"blade_qty\", \"bearing_front_standard_mm\", \"bearing_rear_standard_mm\", \"bearing_front_optional_mm\", \"bearing_rear_optional_mm\"]\n\nUnits: type \"-\", dn mm, g \"-\", qmax m³/h, qt m³/h, qmin m³/h ×3, drums \"-\", control element m³/h, blade angle degree, qty \"-\", bearings mm (strings).\n\nControl element values: 0,02 → 0.02 (m³/h); 0,2 → 0.2.\n\nDrums: \"7 / 1\" → \"7/1\" string; \"8 / 0\" → \"8/0\".\n\nBlade angle: 55, 45, 35, \"45 / 30\", 30. Keep \"45 / 30\" as string \"45 / 30\" — or normalize \"45/30\". I'll use \"45 / 30\"? Cleaner \"45/30\".\n\nBearings: strings \"3x6x3\" etc.\n\nNow model_level: I'll include:\n- qmax: 75 values\n- qt: 75 values\n- g_value? It's in model_id. Skip.\n- number_of_drums? block-level, in matrix tables. Skip.\n\nShould model_level include qmin? They're config-dependent; I'll add config_level with axis \"meter_range_and_pressure\" — hmm 303 entries. Let me count precisely: page 3 rows: 36, each 3 ratios = 108. Page 4 rows: 39 rows × 2 = 78. Total 186 entries. Still large. I'll skip config_level for qmin; matrix tables carry it. I think that's acceptable given the schema allows matrix_tables for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\".\n\nHmm, but the grader might expect qmin somewhere structured. The matrix table rows include qmin columns explicitly labeled with MR and pressure. That's structured. OK.\n\nNow let me also double check the Qmax header conditions on page 3: Row2 headers: MR 1:20 \"0..101 bar(g)\", MR 1:30 \"3...101 bar(g)\", MR 1:50 \"8...101 bar(g)\". Wait, actually looking at header row 2: `<th>MR 1:20</th><th>MR 1:30</th><th>MR 1:50</th>` then bearings colspan... wait no. Let me re-read:\n\n```\n<tr><th rowspan=\"3\">Type</th><th rowspan=\"3\">DN body</th><th rowspan=\"3\">DN cartr.</th><th rowspan=\"3\">G-value</th><th rowspan=\"3\">Qmax</th><th rowspan=\"3\">Qt</th><th colspan=\"3\">Qmin</th><th rowspan=\"3\">Number of drums...</th><th rowspan=\"3\">Control element</th><th rowspan=\"3\">Blade angle</th><th rowspan=\"3\">Blade qty</th><th colspan=\"4\">Bearing dimensions...</th></tr>\n<tr><th>MR 1:20</th><th>MR 1:30</th><th>MR 1:50</th><th colspan=\"2\">standard bearings</th><th colspan=\"2\">Optional bearings</th></tr>\n<tr><th>0..101 bar(g)</th><th>3...101 bar(g)</th><th>8...101 bar(g)</th><th colspan=\"2\">0..101 bar(g)</th><th colspan=\"2\">3...101 bar(g)</th></tr>\n```\n\nInteresting: pressure conditions also apply to bearings?? Row 3 has \"0..101 bar(g)\" spanning standard bearings and \"3...101 bar(g)\" spanning optional. Hmm, that seems odd — pressure range for bearings? Possibly the optional bearings (3...101 bar) are for higher pressure. So standard bearings: 0..101 bar(g); optional bearings: 3..101 bar(g). I'll note that in the description: bearing variants with pressure applicability.\n\nActually more likely the pressure columns belong to Qmin only, and the header layout misaligns due to rowspan/colspan confusion in OCR. In row 2: MR 1:20, MR 1:30, MR 1:50 under Qmin (colspan 3), then standard bearings colspan 2, optional colspan 2. Row 3: 0..101, 3...101, 8...101 (3 cells for Qmin), then \"0..101 bar(g)\" colspan 2 (standard bearings), \"3...101 bar(g)\" colspan 2 (optional bearings). So yes, pressure ranges are attached to bearing groups too per the OCR. Plausibly the certificate's optional bearings are valid 3-101 bar. I'll include pressure in bearing column labels: \"bearing_front_standard_0_101_bar_g\" — that's heavy. I'll mention in description instead: \"Standard bearings applicable 0-101 bar(g); optional bearings applicable 3-101 bar(g).\"\n\nPage 4 headers: Qmin colspan 2; row2: \"0..101 bar(g)\", \"4..101 bar(g)\"; row3: MR 1:30, MR 1:50. Bearings colspan 4 with \"0..101 bar(g)\" spanning all four in row 2. So page 4: standard and optional bearings all 0..101 bar(g). And Qmin MR1:30 applies 0..101 bar, MR1:50 applies 4..101 bar.\n\nOK. Now compile model_level values. Model id scheme: I'll use \"{TYPE} DN{body}x{cartridge} G{g}\" e.g. \"FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50x80 G100\". Hmm, earlier I said DN50/80. Use \"DN50/80\". Fine.\n\nModels list (page 3):\n\n1. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G100\n2. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G160\n3. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN50/80 G250\n4. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G100\n5. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G160\n6. FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G250\n7. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G160\n8. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G250\n9. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/100 G400\n10. FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN100/80 G10