T-Dc, 500, 500), bearings \"20x47x14\",\"10x26x8\",\"20x47x20\",\"10x26x8\":\n64. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130, 45\n65. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200, \"45/30\"\n66. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320, 30\n\nW (FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, 500, 600), bearings \"20x47x20\",\"10x26x8\",\"20x47x20\",\"10x26x8\":\n67. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200, 45\n68. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320, \"45/30\"\n69. G16000: 25000, 5000, 800, 400, 30\n\nX (FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, 600, 500), bearings \"20x47x14\",\"10x26x8\",\"20x47x20\",\"10x26x8\":\n70. G4000: 6500, 1300, 216, 130, 45\n71. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200, \"45/30\"\n72. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320, 30\n\nY (FMT-S, FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc, 600, 600), bearings \"20x47x20\",\"10x26x8\",\"20x47x20\",\"10x26x8\":\n73. G6500: 10000, 2000, 333, 200, 45\n74. G10000: 16000, 3200, 533, 320, \"45/30\"\n75. G16000: 25000, 5000, 800, 400, 30\n\nNote about Qt=80 for G2500 rows: I'll preserve source and add a footnote? Adding a footnote fabricating an assumption is against \"don't fabricate\"? A footnote stating \"Qt for G2500 rows is printed as 80 in the certificate\" is factual description of source. Hmm. Actually maybe it IS 80 in the original (a typo in the original cert). Either way my footnote would be an observation, not a fabrication. I'll skip editorializing to be safe? I think a brief footnote is genuinely useful for downstream QA. I'll add: footnote \"1)\" on those qt cells: \"Printed as '80' in the certificate table.\" Hmm... it risks being wrong if source actually says 800 and OCR misread. The instruction says don't fabricate; footnotes are for extracting footnote definitions. I'll not add footnotes. Just preserve 80.\n\nHmm, wait — actually let me reconsider. If the OCR is right, the cert says 80. If wrong, true value 800. Either way, my job is extraction of the markdown. Preserve 80. Done deliberating.\n\nNow type_level characteristics:\n- measured_quantity: {value: \"Gas volume\", unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null, footnote_markers: []}\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- electromagnetic_class: {value: \"Not applicable (the meter has no electronics)\"}\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min: -40, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- gas_temperature_range: {value: {min: -40, max: 70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- orientation: {value: \"Horizontal / Vertical up / Vertical down (all orientations)\"}\n- flow_direction: {value: \"Uni-directional (indicated with arrow)\"}\n- maximum_operating_pressure? From table headers: 0..101 bar(g). That's pressure ranges tied to Qmin ratios and bearings. Add type_level: max_operating_pressure: {value: 101, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"}? It's inferable: \"101 bar(g)\" appears as upper bound everywhere. It's in the table headers though, not stated as an overall characteristic. I'll include it as type_level with footnote? Risky fabrication? It's directly printed as \"0..101 bar(g)\" for MR1:20 and standard bearings. I'll add characteristic \"pressure_range\" type_level? Hmm — different sub-attributes have different lower bounds (0, 3, 8, 4). Upper 101 common. I'll include maximum_operating_pressure: {value: 101, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"} — justified: all configurations valid up to 101 bar(g). I'll include it. Actually, to be safe, put it in the matrix table description rather than as a fabricated standalone. Hmm. It is genuinely printed in the doc (header cells). I'll include maximum_operating_pressure as type_level. Fine.\n\nAccuracy class 1.0 → in recommendation.accuracy_classes [\"1.0\"].\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Gas meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"FMT-L\", \"FMT-S\", \"FMT-Lx\", \"FMT-Dc\"]\n- module_designation: null? For gas meters, module concept applies (heat meters)... The cert mentions the mechanical index is an interchangeable component. module_designation field: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. I'll set null (no module designation given). Hmm, \"the meter has no electronics\" — I'll use null.\n- description: \"Turbine gas meter\" (from \"A turbine gas meter\").\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"FMT\" (the types are FMT-*; is \"FMT\" explicit as family name? The table is \"General characteristics of the family of instruments\". FMT is the common prefix. I'll set family_name \"FMT turbine gas meter family\"? Don't fabricate — \"FMT\" prefix is explicit. I'll use \"FMT\".)\n\nNow issuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"the Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: null (not given)\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R137/2012-A-NL1-25.03\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3972617\"\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2025-07-18\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants: [{name: \"Flow Meter Group B.V.\", address_lines: [\"Innovatieweg 32\", \"7007 CD Doetinchem\", \"The Netherlands\"]}]\nmanufacturers: same (Applicant and Manufacturer).\n\nrecommendation: {id: \"R137-1\", edition: 2012, amendment: null, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"1.0\"]}\n\nHmm accuracy class \"1,0\" — convert to \"1.0\". OK.\n\ntest_reports:\n1. {id: \"NMi-14200104-01\", date: \"2014-09-15\", pages: 14, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n2. {id: \"NMi-14200702-01\", date: \"2014-12-18\", pages: 13, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n3. {id: \"NMi-14200712-01\", date: \"2014-12-23\", pages: 35, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n4. {id: \"VSL-3255499-01\", date: \"2015-01-28\", pages: 6, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n5. {id: \"NMi-1901860-02\", date: \"2018-04-26\", pages: 4, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n6. {id: \"T10466-7\", role: \"documentation_file\"} — date/pages not present, omit.\n7. {id: \"10466/6-03\", role: \"documentation_file\"}\n8. {id: \"10466/7-05\", role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nShould I include 6-8? They're documentation referenced. I'll include T10466-7 and the two installation docs. Fine.\n\nrevision_history: [{revision: \"Initial\", date: \"2025-07-18\", changes: \"This OIML certificate replaces the R137/2012-B-NL1-18.05 certificate released under scheme B.\"}]\n\ncomponents: mechanical index with alternatives.\n\nconfig_level: installation straight pipe lengths:\n[\n {attribute: \"straight_inlet_pipe_length\", unit_symbol: \"DN\", axis: \"meter model / flow straightener\", values: [\n   {condition: \"FMT-L with large flow straightener\", value: 1},\n   {condition: \"FMT-L with small flow straightener\", value: 5},\n   {condition: \"FMT-S (large flow straightener only)\", value: 1},\n   {condition: \"FMT-Lx\", value: 0},\n   {condition: \"FMT-Dc\", value: 0}\n ]}\n]\n\nValues as structured objects: rule 2 strict — {condition, value, unit_symbol}. The config_level values shape per schema: \"values[]: {condition, value}\". Rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". I'll include unit_symbol \"DN\" at entry level and value numeric.\n\nFor FMT-Lx: \"no installation requirements concerning straight inlet tubing\" → value 0. OK.\n\nAlso mention flow straightener doc numbers in the condition or a note. I'll add description text in matrix? I'll put doc numbers into the component? Better: add them into the config values condition: \"FMT-L with large flow straightener (docs 10466/6-03, 10466/7-05)\" — ambiguous mapping, so phrase: \"FMT-L with large flow straightener (one of doc. 10466/6-03 / 10466/7-05)\". Clunky. Alternative: add a footnotes entry? footnotes are for footnote markers. I'll add a matrix_tables? No. I'll add a \"components\" entry for flow straighteners:\n\ncomponents: [\n {role: \"flow straightener (FMT-L, optional; FMT-S standard)\", type_designations: [\"10466/6-03\", \"10466/7-05\"], alternatives: \"OR\", characteristics: {}},\n ...\n]\n\nHmm, doc numbers aren't type designations. But they identify the two straightener variants. Acceptable with a note? I'll include with alternatives OR and characteristics: {\"documentation_numbers\": ...}. Let me structure:\n\n{\n  \"role\": \"flow straightener\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"10466/6-03\", \"10466/7-05\"],\n  \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"applicability\": {\"value\": \"FMT-L: choice of two; FMT-S: large straightener only; FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc: inlet flow straightener identical to FMT-S\", ...}\n  }\n}\n\nKeep it simple. And straight-pipe config_level as above.\n\nNow the mechanical index component:\n\n{\n  \"role\": \"mechanical index (interchangeable component)\",\n  \"type_designations\": [],\n  \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"pulse_output\": {\"value\": \"reed contact / Wiegand / encoder\", ...}\n  }\n}\n\nHmm \"alternatives: OR\" at component level refers to alternatives between components entries. With a single entry, alternatives refers to the type_designations/pulse options. I'll set alternatives: \"OR\" and type_designations: [\"reed contact\", \"Wiegand\", \"encoder\"] — these are the alternative equipment options. That reads naturally.\n\nNow let me think about whether to include model_level g_value, drums etc. I'll include:\n- g_value? in model_id. skip.\n- maximum_flow_rate (qmax): 75 values\n- transitional_flow_rate (qt): 75 values\n- minimum_flow_rate? config-dependent. Skip (in matrix tables).\n- number_of_drums_before_and_after_decimal_point? block-level; skip (in tables).\n- control_element? skip.\n- blade_angle, blade_qty? row-level; skip (tables).\n\nAttribute naming: canonical snake_case. \"qmax\" is fine as canonical for gas meters. I'll use \"qmax\", \"qt\".\n\nUnit \"m³/h\" — unit_symbol \"m³/h\", unit_id maybe \"u:cubic_metre_per_hour\"? Rule: leave null unless certain. I'll leave null.\n\nNow writing all this JSON. Size concern: 75 matrix rows with 17 keys each. Let me write compactly but valid JSON.\n\nAlso \"column_units\" — schema example includes it. I'll include.\n\nLet me also double check drums column for blocks F,H,J? Block F row 3 (G1000) has drums \"8 / 0\" and control \"0,2\" — note in markdown for block F, the drums/control cells: rows 1-2 have rowspan 2 \"7 / 1\" and \"0,02\"; row 3 has its own \"8 / 0\", \"0,2\". Yes as I have. Block I: row 1 has own 7/1 0,02; rows 2-3 share rowspan2 8/0 0,2. Yes. Block L rows: row1 own 7/1 0,02; rows2-3 8/0 0,2. ✓.\n\nWait, check block I markdown: \n```\n<tr><td rowspan=\"3\">FMT-Lx or FMT-Dc</td><td rowspan=\"3\">150</td><td rowspan=\"3\">200</td><td>650</td><td>1000</td><td>200</td><td>50</td><td>32</td><td>20</td><td>7 / 1</td><td>0,02</td><td>55</td><td>12</td>...\n<tr><td>1000</td><td>1600</td><td>320</td><td>80</td><td>50</td><td>32</td><td rowspan=\"2\">8 / 0</td><td rowspan=\"2\">0,2</td><td>45</td><td>14</td>\n<tr><td>1600</td><td>2500</td><td>500</td><td>125</td><td>80</td><td>50</td><td>35</td><td>16</td>\n```\nYes.\n\nCheck block B row3 G250: `<tr><td>250</td><td>400</td><td>80</td><td>20</td><td>12,5</td><td>8</td><td>35</td><td>16</td></tr>` — qmin 20/12.5/8, blade 35/16 ✓.\n\nPage 3 block F row 2 (G650): `<tr><td>650</td><td>1000</td><td>200</td><td>50</td><td>32</td><td>20</td><td>45</td><td>14</td></tr>` ✓.\n\nNow decide the model_id format. Long ones like \"FMT-L/FMT-S/FMT-Lx/FMT-Dc DN80/80 G100\". Fine.\n\nAlternatively shorter: include a \"types\" attribute in model entry and id \"DN80/80 G100\"? But same DN/G may map to different type sets across tables (e.g., DN250/250 only in FMT-S..., DN300/300 only FMT-S...; DN80/80 is FMT-L..; DN100/80 only FMT-Lx/Dc). Any collision? DN200/200: FMT-L,FMT-S,FMT-Lx,FMT-Dc G650/G1000/G1600 — unique. DN250/200 FMT-Lx/Dc G650-1600 (page3) vs DN250/250 page4 — different cartridges. Looks unique with DN+G. But keep types in id for clarity anyway. Long ids repeated in model_level → more tokens. Hmm, tokens: model_id avg ~40 chars → in qmax values list 75×(~12 tokens) ≈ 900 tokens; qt same ≈ 900. OK.\n\nAlternatively skip model_level qt? The R137 example had qmax, qt, qmin